course: dictionary
lesson: 8432
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The clues in the sentence tell you which meaning the writer wants. «In medieval Europe a sack of pepper could be worth a fortune.» The word pepper and the fortune tell you that a sack is a big bag full of pepper.
explain: «The company decided to sack two of its workers.» Here sack is a verb, something that is done to people. It means the boss tells them they no longer have their jobs. Always ask: what would fit in this sentence? Then put your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: «After sports day, the tired children hit the sack.» Hit the sack is a phrase, and nobody is hitting anything. The clue is tired, so it means they went to bed. Phrases like this do not mean what each word means on its own, so read the whole group of words.
fact: A sack is a big strong bag for carrying things like potatoes, flour or coal.
fact: «Sack» can also be a verb: to sack someone means to tell them they no longer have their job.
fact: «Get the sack» means lose your job.
fact: «Hit the sack» means go to bed.
fact: In Italian, «un sacco» (a sack) means «loads», like English «a ton», so «un sacco di bene» means «a lot of love».
exercise: «The farmer filled a sack with potatoes.» What is a sack here? | A kind of race | *A big strong bag for carrying things | A bed for sleeping in | A type of fruit
exercise: Ben says, «My dad got the sack today.» What has happened to his dad? | He has been given a bag of flour | *He has lost his job | He has started a holiday | He has gone to bed early
exercise: In a «sack race», what do the children do? | Sing a song | *Hop in a sack to the finish line | Swim across the pool | Read a book
test: «The oranges were packed into sacks after the harvest.» What does sacks mean here? | *Big strong bags | Sweets | Beds | Flowers
test: Sofia says, «I'm going to hit the sack, I'm exhausted.» What does she mean? | She is going to sell her bag | She is going to fight someone | She is going to play football | *She is going to bed
exam: Which sentence uses sack as a verb? | The sack was heavy with flour. | *The company had to sack two of its workers. | She put the sack of coal by the door. | We ran the sack race on sports day.
